mptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of CCD relies on several assumptions:\n- The relationship between the factors and the response is approximately quadratic.\n- The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n- The observations are independent.\n- The factors are continuous and can be varied smoothly.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nCCD is applicable when:\n- The researcher seeks to optimize a response variable influenced by multiple factors.\n- A preliminary understanding of the factor effects is available, typically from a factorial design.\n- The goal is to explore the curvature of the response surface.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data obtained from a CCD typically involves:\n1. Fitting the second-order polynomial model using regression analysis.\n2. Evaluating the significance of the model and individual coefficients using ANOVA.\n3. Conducting diagnostic checks for model assumptions.\n4. Using response surface plots to visualize the effects and identify optimal conditions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- CCD is efficient in terms of the number of runs required compared to a full three-level factorial design.\n- It provides a comprehensive understanding of the response surface, including curvature.\n- The design is flexible and can be adapted to various experimental conditions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- CCD may not be suitable for all types of response surfaces, particularly if the true relationship is not quadratic.\n- The design can become complex with an increasing number of factors, leading to a larger number of runs.\n- The choice of the axial distance \\( \\alpha \\) can influence the design's properties and should be carefully considered.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include Box-Behnken designs and full factorial designs. Alternatives to CCD may involve using D-optimal designs or other response surface methodologie